Skin Cancer Detection (CNN)

A convolutional neural network for binary classification of skin lesion images as benign or malignant.

## Dataset

2,637 training images and 660 test images across two classes (Benign, Malignant), with data augmentation applied during training (shear, zoom, rotation, brightness, vertical flip).

## Model

A CNN built with Keras/TensorFlow:
- 4 convolutional blocks (Conv2D + MaxPooling), with increasing filter depth (32 → 64 → 128 → 256)
- Dropout layers for regularization
- Batch normalization
- Trained with early stopping on validation loss

## Results

Test accuracy: 82.6%

This is a moderate result for a binary medical imaging task. It reflects a working end-to-end CNN pipeline (data loading, augmentation, training, evaluation, inference) rather than a clinically usable classifier. No precision/recall/F1 breakdown was captured for this run — for a medical classification task, false negatives (missing a malignant case) matter more than raw accuracy, so that breakdown would be a meaningful next step rather than an optional add-on.

## Known Limitations & Next Steps

- No precision/recall/F1 or confusion matrix currently reported — accuracy alone is not sufficient for a medical classification task
- Transfer learning (VGG16/VGG19/Xception were imported but not used) would likely improve performance over a CNN trained from scratch on ~2.6K images
- File paths in the notebook are hardcoded to a local machine and would need to be parameterized to run elsewhere

